course: dictionary
lesson: 7150
title: Percussion: things you hit, shake or scrape
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Percussion is a family of instruments. You play them by hitting, shaking or scraping them, so the sound starts with a knock, a shake or a scrape. Drums, tambourines, triangles, cymbals and xylophones are all percussion instruments.
explain: Percussion can also name the players. «Orchestras usually seat the quieter strings at the front and the louder brass and percussion at the back, to balance the sound.» Here percussion means the players at the back who play those instruments.
explain: Use the clues around the word. Ask how the instrument makes its sound: strings shake strings, brass players buzz their lips, but percussion is always hit, shaken or scraped. If your meaning fits the sentence, you are right.
fact: Percussion means musical instruments that you play by hitting, shaking or scraping them.
fact: Percussion is one of the four orchestra families: strings, woodwind, brass and percussion.
fact: «Body percussion» means clapping, clicking and stamping to make music.
fact: Some percussion instruments, like the xylophone and the timpani, are tuned and play real notes.
fact: Percussion can also mean a sharp knock, but this is a formal use of the word.
exercise: What do you do to make a percussion instrument sound? | Buzz your lips into it | Press a bow across its strings | Blow across an edge | *Hit, shake or scrape it
exercise: «The percussion section kept the beat.» What does percussion section mean here? | *The players of the drums, cymbals and other percussion | The conductor and the singers | A group of dancers | The strings sitting at the front
exercise: Which of these is NOT a percussion instrument? | Triangle | Tambourine | Xylophone | *Violin
test: Body percussion means | Blowing into a trumpet | Singing a high note | Playing the violin with a bow | *Clapping, clicking and stamping to make music
test: «The timpani are big orchestra drums tuned to notes.» Why are they in the percussion family? | They are made of wood, like a flute | Their sound is made by blowing across an edge | They are shaken by the player's hand | *They are hit to make their sound
exam: «A piano has strings struck by hammers when you press keys, so people argue whether it is strings or percussion.» Why do people argue about the piano? | It is shaken like a tambourine | *Its strings are struck by hammers, so it fits two families | It has no strings at all | It is always played with a bow
